---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/alternatives/tinyemail-alternative';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'alternatives/tinyemail-alternative');
const desc = description(site, 'alternatives/tinyemail-alternative');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When is staying with tinyEmail reasonable?",
    "answer": "Keep it when campaign production, templates and automation meet the team\u2019s needs. Repair the specific catalog or contact mapping defect before paying to migrate it."
  },
  {
    "question": "What belongs in the handoff?",
    "answer": "Assign owners to campaign schedules, template assets, catalog feeds, contact permissions, suppressions and every form or integration that writes contacts. Reconcile source and destination counts separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can templates simply be copied?",
    "answer": "Treat exported HTML and assets as inputs for reconstruction. Verify merge fields, dynamic product blocks, unsubscribe links and mobile rendering in the receiving editor."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much does the retained discount cost?",
    "answer": "At $795 revenue and 70% gross margin, fixed goods cost is $238.50. A 13% discount reduces revenue to $691.65 and gross profit from $556.50 to $453.15, losing $103.35 before other costs."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-16 container">
<a href="/alternatives" class="link">← All alternatives</a>
<article class="prose-shopify mt-10">
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">tinyEmail alternatives: transfer campaign ownership without losing cart context</h1>
<p>A replacement needs to preserve the merchandising team’s ability to ship a correct campaign, not just import addresses. Use a campaign, catalog and contact handoff to decide whether switching solves a real operating constraint.</p>
<h2>Keep the useful production tools in the decision</h2>
<p>Checked November 25, 2026: <a href="https://www.tinyemail.com/">tinyEmail’s product site</a> describes campaigns, automations, segmentation and a REST API. Its campaign editor and template workflow can be valuable to a team producing regular merchandising emails. These are reasons to include the existing setup in the evaluation rather than assume it has shallow automation. Trial the specific workflow you need; supplier demonstrations do not establish results for your store.</p>
<h2>Give each campaign asset an owner</h2>
<p>Before changing the integration, record drafts, recurring campaigns, scheduled sends, sender identities, approval owners, image locations and template versions. Keep the source originals accessible. Mark each item retained, rebuilt or retired, with a destination identifier and reviewer. A copied HTML file is not proof that the receiving editor can maintain it.</p>
<p>Rebuild one ordinary newsletter and one cart reminder using actual catalog shapes in a test audience. Check missing first names, long product titles, sale prices, currency, variant images, alt text, mobile layout and unsubscribe links. The campaign owner should be able to revise the product block without an engineer. If a proprietary block cannot move, estimate reconstruction work before accepting the quote.</p>
<h2>Reconcile catalog and contact writers</h2>
<p>List the Shopify sync, forms, API callers and manual imports that create or update contacts. Assign one owner to each writer and document where subscription status wins over a later profile update. Export available contact identifiers, source and timestamp evidence, preferences, suppressions and custom attributes; keep ambiguous records excluded from affected sends. Reconcile sendable, suppressed and unresolved counts independently instead of treating every imported address as subscribed.</p>
<p>Have the merchandising owner compare product and variant IDs, collection membership, destination URLs and inventory freshness across the old and proposed product blocks. Change a variant price and remove another from sale before rendering a queued reminder. A stale template cache must not advertise a discontinued item. Decide who corrects a broken catalog feed and what message is withheld while it is stale.</p>
<h2>Choose a trial by the work it enables</h2>
<p>For a Shopify event and segmentation workflow, examine <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/klaviyo-email-marketing">Klaviyo</a>; for forms and prebuilt ecommerce recovery workflows, examine <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/omnisend">Omnisend</a>. Their listings describe those capabilities. Ask each operator to rebuild the same two campaign assets and explain purchaser exits and exclusions. Compare editor effort, permissions, required channel costs and parallel operation with repairing tinyEmail. Neither is a universal winner, and adding an SMS sender is a separate decision requiring its own permission and suppression handoff.</p>
<h2>Acceptance exercises for a real cart</h2>
<p>These are proposed exercises, not performed migrations or measured outcomes. Create a cart with two variants, then change quantity and purchase on another device. Replay the original event after the order: the receiving workflow should recognize the completed purchase and avoid a fresh recovery enrollment. Record the cart/order identifiers, event times, decision and responsible operator. If purchase state is unavailable, hold the reminder rather than rely on a fixed delay as a safety guarantee.</p>
<p>For a sold-out size, remove the stale purchase link or withhold the affected message; a discount cannot restore stock. For gift sizing, use a working size guide and support contact. For a wholesale minimum quantity, explain the actual requirement and verify it at checkout. For shipping shock, display the applicable delivery cost and region; a generic percentage code may leave the real objection unresolved.</p>
<p>On mobile, reopen the saved cart with its correct variants and quantities, then check a failed financing or payment return path without exposing payment data. Compare an existing bundle price and automatic discount with the recovery code: verify stacking eligibility and the final checkout total before publishing the template. A repeat customer already in a welcome or promotional campaign needs a documented collision rule, not an automatic extra sequence.</p>
<h2>Keep the discount calculation honest</h2>
<p>The retained illustration uses $795 revenue at 70% gross margin: fixed goods cost $238.50 and gross profit $556.50. A 13% discount removes $103.35 revenue, leaving $691.65 revenue and $453.15 gross profit. Shipping, payment fees, returns and messaging costs are excluded. Compare the final checkout basket, including bundles and shipping subsidy, rather than subtracting the discount from profit as a percentage. Attributed recovery revenue does not establish incremental profit; an appropriately sized comparison cohort is a proposed measurement design, not an observed result.</p>
<h2>Cut over the production calendar</h2>
<p>Freeze edits for an agreed handoff window, reconcile pending campaigns and cart enrollments, and disable the old scheduler before enabling its replacement. Keep a ledger of canceled, completed and deliberately recreated sends so a replay cannot duplicate a newsletter or reminder. Test an unsubscribe after import and confirm that every active writer preserves it.</p>
<p>Require campaign-owner approval of rendering and catalog accuracy and data-owner approval of counts and exclusions. If either fails, pause the receiving sends and fix the mapping. Rollback restores only the explicitly retained scheduler and current permission state; do not reactivate canceled campaigns or overwrite new opt-outs with an old export. Save the acceptance evidence and unresolved asset list before removing access to tinyEmail.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="FAQ: leave tinyEmail?" />
</section>
</BaseLayout>
